          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ×
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\times n
            matrices with entries in a unital ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            form a unital ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Mat
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ×
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mat_{n \times n}(R)
            with unit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_n
            (diagonal matrix whose each entry at the main diagonal is
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi Mat
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ×
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\in Mat_{n \times n}(R)
            is invertible (also said regular) if it has a two-sided inverse
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^{-1}
            in that unital ring, called the matrix inverse (or inverse matrix) of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi I
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A A^{-1} = I_n = A^{-1} A
          %h2#variants Variants
          %h4#onesided_inverses One-sided inverses
          %p
            Sometimes one-sided inverses in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Mat
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ×
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mat_{n \times n}(R)
            are also useful as well as one-sided inverses of rectangular matrices.
          %h4#schur_complement Schur complement
          %p
            Sometimes, an inverse matrix does not exist but formulas for some entries of inverse matrix make sense. Related expressions include
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasideterminant"} quasideterminant
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schur+complement"} Schur complement
          %h4#moorepenrose_inverse Moore–Penrose inverse
          %p
            A generalized inverse
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^{-1}
            satisfying weaker requirement
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A A^{-1} A = I_n
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi A
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^{-1} = A^{-1} A A^{-1}
            is sometimes useful, especially in applied mathematics and approximation theory. These identities play role also in
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+semigroup"} inverse semigroup
          %p In the case of matrices, such generalized inverse is known as Moore–Penrose inverse.
          %h2#properties Properties
          #FundamentalTheoremOfLinearAlgebra.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              %strong (fundamental theorem of invertible matrices)
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo =
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R = k
              is a field,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi Mat
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ×
                        %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in Mat_{n \times n}(k)
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square+matrix"} square matrix
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                  is an invertible matrix;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                  is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+product"} matrix product
                  of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+matrices"} elementary matrices
